  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "nqXTOhrucwBJjEIRK25NEGvvsjk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:39:33.855Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`useDocumentSafe` was added in v6.0.0 to make the loading and failure states of a document read into ordinary render-time values:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useDocumentSafe } from \"@powerhousedao/reactor-browser\";\n\nfunction MyEditor({ documentId }: { documentId: string }) {\n  const { document, error, isLoading } = useDocumentSafe(documentId);\n  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;\n  if (error) return <ErrorBanner error={error} />;\n  return <Editor document={document} />;\n}\n```\n\nThe release notes describe it as replacing \"the pattern of calling `useDocument` and catching thrown errors \u2014 now surfaces error and loading state as first-class values.\"\n\nThe reason the old pattern was bad is specific to React rather than to Powerhouse: a hook that throws cannot be caught by a `try/catch` around the call, because the throw happens during render. Handling it meant an error boundary, and an error boundary is a poor fit for \"this document is still loading\" or \"this document id doesn't resolve\" \u2014 those are expected states of a working editor, not crashes. Returning a discriminated result puts the branch back where the component can express it.\n\nThis is the same design distinction that runs through the rest of the hook surface: throwing variants for cases where absence is a programming error, `Safe` variants for cases where absence is legitimate. Reach for the `Safe` variant whenever \"no document yet\" is a state your UI should be able to render.\n\nProvenance: academy `ReleaseNotes/v6.0.0.md` (\"useDocumentSafe Hook\"), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:39:32.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:39:33.855Z"
    }
  },
